<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chao Phraya River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chao Phraya is the main river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, formed at Nakhon Sawan by the meeting of the Ping and the Nan and flowing south past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. Its fertile flood plain supported the Dvaravati towns, Lavo and Suphannaphum, and the successive Thai capitals of Ayutthaya, Thonburi and Bangkok, whose wealth rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and on trade carried along the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river.jpg" alt="Chao Phraya River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chao_Phraya_River_-_Bangkok_-_northwards_-_from_King_Taksin_Bridge_-_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I. He built his capital on an island formed where the Lopburi and Pa Sak rivers meet the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, a site that could be defended by water in the flood season, commanded the rice lands of the central plain and lay within reach of seagoing ships from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Taksin, a provincial governor of partly Chinese descent, rallied resistance, expelled the occupying garrisons and ruled from Thonburi on the west bank of the <b>Chao Phraya River</b>, but by 1782 his rule had become erratic and a revolt broke out in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sukhothai lies on the Yom River, a tributary of the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, in the plain of north-central Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Having moved his capital that year to an island in the <b>Chao Phraya River</b>, where he founded Ayutthaya, Ramathibodi I was formally established as ruler of a centralized state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Moated towns appear in the <b>Chao Phraya</b> valley from around the 6th and 7th centuries, built by Mon-speaking communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a></li><li><blockquote>As Khmer power waned in the 13th century, Lavo was linked by marriage and politics with Suphannaphum, and in 1351 U Thong founded the Ayutthaya Kingdom on the <b>Chao Phraya</b>, combining both.</blockquote><a href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lavo Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
